WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.290
There’s a country that always, always, always
appears in the headlines of international

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.280
newspapers for two main reasons: soccer and
economic crises.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.700
The problem, my friends, is that lately, these
headlines are having more to do with crises

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.980
than with football

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.520
Of course, I’m talking about Argentina.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:30.000
This is hard to say, but Argentina is a country
that seems as allergic to reforms as it is

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.570
addicted to crises.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:37.960
When everything seemed to be going well, 2018
suddenly became very complicated-- so much

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.050
so that Mauricio Macri’s government has
been forced to ask for a "bailout" from the

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.290
International Monetary Fund.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.950
Wait, a bailout?

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.260
In Argentina?

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.260
Again?

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.580
Well, yes... you heard that right.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.620
Argentina, the country of tango is back on
the ropes.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.360
Listen up.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:09.800
April and May of 2018 haven’t precisely
been good months for Argentina.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:41.600
In just a few weeks, the peso was depreciated
by 20%, going from an exchange rate of 1 US

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.560
dollar for 20 pesos to a 1 US dollar for 25
pesos.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:49.480
But...

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.490
You know what?

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.100
This isn’t surprising.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:58.359
Since the beginning of 2018, Argentina’s
currency has depreciated by 25%...

00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:03.759
And we extended the time to the last 5 years...
could you tell me how much the Argentine peso

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:04.759
depreciated?

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.580
Well... by no less than 80%.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.620
Now, make no mistake, the plummeting exchange
rate isn’t the problem... but the consequence.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.239
See, my friends, Argentina’s real problem
lies in its addiction to public spending…

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:37.620
And I’m not saying that public spending
is slightly higher than they can afford…

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.719
they have such a high public spending rate
that Argentina simply cannot afford it.

00:02:43.719 --> 00:02:49.790
(AUDIO: “In the last 57 years, we’ve spent
53 years with the State spending more than

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:55.609
they collect, that is with a fiscal deficit,
during those 53 years we had 4 huge crises.

00:02:55.609 --> 00:03:01.300
Now we’re fighting to avoid a fifth one”.-
José Luis Espert, economist and author of

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.340
the book “The Devoured Argentina”)

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.200
But... wait a second…

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:05.209
I know what you may be thinking...

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:10.459
Simon, what the hell does public spending
have to do with the peso’s depreciation?

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:12.340
Well... much more than you think.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.100
Let’s go through it step by step.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:31.840
Argentina is facing a triple time bomb: it
has a huge external deficit, a huge public

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.159
deficit and 250 billion dollars of external
debt.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:41.060
Of course, as you can imagine... both the
deficit and debt have to be paid...

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.360
And here’s where the first big problem appears:

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:52.599
Years and years of protectionism, devaluations,
hyper regulation and very high taxes – Because

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:59.629
Argentina is one of the countries with the
highest business taxes on the entire planet--All

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:04.779
of this has stopped the industry, and the
Argentine economy in general, from being competitive...

00:04:04.779 --> 00:04:13.969
and has caused capitalists to flee the country
long ago in search of refuge.

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:14.969
Sounds logical, right?

00:04:14.969 --> 00:04:17.380
Well, the result?

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:18.910
Since there’s no "money" at home...

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:22.370
Argentina has become very dependent on foreign
financing.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:27.930
According to the Institute of International
Finance, most, almost 64% of Argentina’s

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.980
state and corporate debt is denominated in
dollars and other foreign currencies.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:39.360
That’s why both the volume of Central Bank
reserves and the exchange rate are very, very

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.040
important.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.920
Of course, on the one hand, they need to pay
for imports and deal with debt payments...

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:53.280
But... since Argentina has a negative external
balance--that is, it consumes more dollars

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.270
than it gets... it needs someone to lend them
those dollars.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:01.620
It needs investors to bring money to the country.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.100
And, my friends, if investors don’t lend
that money because they don’t trust the

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.910
country… then Argentina has a problem, a
huge problem.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.120
On the other hand, if the peso depreciates
then... obviously, it loses value... and,

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.800
folks, let's be honest, nobody wants to have
their savings in a currency that devalues

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:20.800
so quickly...

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.639
What do you do in that situation?

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:39.080
Well, you rush to exchange all your pesos
for a “stronger” and more “stable”

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.080
currency…

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:46.050
And that’s exactly what Argentines are doing,
which is causing the peso to collapse even

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:50.160
more... and for the reserves to be consumed
much faster.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.150
But... okay, having said this.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.960
Let's take a look at the government.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.759
See, both Néstor Kirchner and especially
Cristina Fernández de Kirchner, the two previous

00:06:02.759 --> 00:06:06.830
presidents of Argentina, they liked to spend
quite freely...

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:12.830
When they came to power in 2002, public spending
was equivalent to 23% of the GDP, when they

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.550
left... it was more than 40%.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:18.880
But you’re surely wondering... what did
they do with all that money?

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.190
Well basically... they hired public employees
- To win votes, you know - and multiplied

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:24.180
subsidies.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.940
Thus, for example, in only 13 years, public
employment soared by 60%.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.660
To give you an idea of how extreme this was,

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:37.820
today 35% of Argentine employees work for the goverment.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:04.539
And get this.

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:10.169
In seven provinces there are even more public
workers than private workers.

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:11.440
Crazy!

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.820
And of course, my friends, given that the
government didn’t have enough money to pay

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.580
for all this well... year after year they
registered a huge public deficit.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.729
However... as you may know... the Kirchners
couldn’t go to the international markets,

00:07:30.729 --> 00:07:35.349
first because their investors didn’t trust
them, and second because the country was already

00:07:35.349 --> 00:07:36.500
in default…

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.320
And, of course, if you don’t pay, obviously,
investors don’t lend you money again.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.290
So, what do you think they did?

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.290
Exactly!

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:15.070
They used every populist politician’s favorite
machine: the money printing machine to pay

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:16.840
public expenditures with new pesos.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.480
That and establishing a currency cap, which
means limiting the amount of money that Argentines

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:29.700
could take out of the country... so they wouldn’t
eat up their dwindling reserves.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.480
And the question is, what happens when these
policies continue?

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:40.530
Well... inflation soars due to all the new
pesos in circulation... and foreign investors,

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.080
of course, don’t want to come near you.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:47.620
And so, it was in these circumstances that
Mauricio Macri came to power.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.500
(THE MACRI PLAN)

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:03.520
When Mauricio Macri came to the
Casa Rosada he had a plan: to implement reforms

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.580
little by little... commonly known as gradualism…

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.540
And the truth is that they’ve been quite
successful:

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.890
the business climate has been improved, some
subsidies have been reduced and, in a way,

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:30.780
they regained the international investors’
trust.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:40.040
But... out of all the hard things they needed
to do, they’ve only done the easiest ones.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.140
What they haven’t achieved in more than
two years of governing, has been decreasing

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.320
the public deficit or inflation.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:54.320
And in spite of having access to the financial
markets, the Argentine government has continued

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:59.420
to print bills to maintain their huge public
expenditure... and that of course means more

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:00.420
inflation.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:04.390
This chart shows how the money is growing,
that is, how the Central Bank of the Republic

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:08.600
of Argentina continues to issue more and more
pesos.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:25.580
But... okay, you may be wondering, wasn’t
Mauricio Macri supposed to change things?

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.260
Yes... but slowly.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.780
See, this all has an explanation.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.320
Macri's team’s idea was to carry out reforms
little by little, to attract investments and

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:37.230
make the Argentine economy grow.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:41.310
If the economy grew, the government would
raise more money and wouldn't have to make

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:42.300
cuts.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:49.471
Meanwhile, little by little, as they regained
the international markets’ trust, they would

00:10:49.471 --> 00:10:52.970
be able to issue a debt and thus reduce the
need to print bills.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:57.620
In this way, within a few years Argentina
would get rid of their deficit, inflation

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:58.950
and would grow again.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:03.870
And Macri, of course, would maintain his political
capital to continue winning elections.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.400
Sounds good, right?

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.060
Well, so far, more or less, things have gone
as planned.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:19.980
But just a moment, because that has now changed,
mainly for two reasons.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:27.450
First... after two and a half years in power,
it’s hard to understand how the public deficit,

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:33.820
inflation and taxes continue to skyrocket...
gradualism has become something like racing

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:35.360
a turtle with rheumatism.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:43.180
And second... because the Federal Reserve
of the United States has begun to raise interests.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:48.980
See, for public debt in the United States,
a 10-year bond is already yielding 3%...

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:59.920
And of course many investors now ask themselves:

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:04.490
If the US debt, which is supposedly the less
risky asset, gives me a reasonable return,

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:10.780
why on earth would I risk lending my money
to Argentina, where I see no changes?

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:16.600
Well... that’s exactly what has caused many
capitals which had begun to show interest

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:20.360
in Argentina, to turn around.

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:21.360
And for this reason...

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:25.190
Macri’s government has decided to resort
to the International Monetary Fund to obtain

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.710
a credit line with which to face the debt
maturities of the upcoming months and the

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:34.271
future.... lest investors no longer want to
put more money on the table.

00:12:34.271 --> 00:12:38.440
Can you imagine if a new default occurred
before the 2019 elections?

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:39.440
See?

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:41.010
Everything has to do with the government’s
behavior.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.320
And if you think about it for a moment, it's
very sensible.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.020
The patience once held for Argentina is gone.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.980
What investors and Argentine savers themselves
say is..

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:49.980
“Fine!

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.920
If you want to spend more than you can, good
for you, but not with my money”.

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:03.360
And not only in regard to public spending,
but many other reforms that make it easier

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:04.490
to do business.

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:06.550
And this brings up a key question...

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:09.120
Is it time to abandon gradualism?

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:20.300
(AGGRESSIVE THERAPIES OR GRADUALISM?)

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:26.440
Folks, making reforms is never easy... but
I think it’s time for the Casa Rosada - the

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:29.330
Argentinian White House - to ask itself a
question.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:32.440
Shouldn’t they try employing more aggressive
measures?

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:33.640
Well... you know what?

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:35.950
First let’s take a look at the historical
evidence.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:41.220
Which do you think has worked better, gradualism,
a calm, step-by-step change, or aggressive

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:42.300
therapy?

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:47.100
Well... we can see a good example of huge
transformations in the former countries of

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.240
the Soviet orbit.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:53.130
When the communist model collapsed in 1989,
many countries employed drastic changes, almost

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:56.080
overnight, while others opted for more gradual
measures.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:58.660
And you know who had the best results?

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.940
Yes, the ones that made the most reforms.

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:11.720
Countries such as Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania,
Poland and the Czech Republic opted for a

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:15.960
more drastic change, that is, making reforms
as quickly as possible.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:24.910
And yes, it was hard at the beginning, but
soon they began to grow and achieved all kinds

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.920
of investments, to such an extent that today
these countries, which were mostly poor in

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:34.080
1989, have a higher living standard than Portugal
or any other Latin American country.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:41.740
On the other hand, those who applied gradual
reforms... countries such as Ukraine, Romania,

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:47.760
Tajikistan and Armenia... let’s say, things
haven’t gone so well, neither in economic

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.730
nor in social terms.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:55.660
Anyway... folks, this is the decision that
Macri’s government has to make.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:15:00.310
Whether or not to take advantage of the IMF's
money to carry out strong reforms once and

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:01.310
for all...

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:06.890
And let's be clear, if Argentina doesn’t
fix its public deficit and stops printing

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:10.450
bills, the future doesn’t look very good
for them.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:12.761
“Borrowing money isn’t good governance.”

00:15:12.761 --> 00:15:18.740
“Good governance is creating basic reforms:
in Argentina there’s an elephantine, gigantic,

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.880
hypertrophied state that needs to slim down.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.310
They need to cut public spending.”

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:25.410
- Jaime Bayly.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:30.280
In addition, the model simply doesn’t work:
the Argentine government had never spent so

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:35.430
much, and despite that, it’s been decades
since there was so much poverty and so much

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:36.320
violence.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:39.030
Will Macri listen?

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:40.190
Only the future will tell.

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.030
But now it's your turn: What do you think?

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:46.440
Should they continue with the gradual reforms
or is it time for a faster change?

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:56.090
Leave your answer in the comments as well as in this servey.

